    <td><small>phase transitions</small></td>
    <td><small>emergence</small></td>
  </tr>
  <tr align=center>
    <td bgcolor="BLUE"><b>Complex Systems</b></td>
    <td><small>cellular automata</small></td>
    <td><small>growth models</small></td>
    <td><small>phase transitions</small></td>
    <td bgcolor="#ffffff">---</td>
    <td><small>self-organization</small></td>
  </tr>
  <tr align=center>
    <td bgcolor="BLUE"><b>Adaptation</b></td>
    <td><small>coadaptation</small></td>
    <td><small>hierarchical models</small></td>
    <td><small>emergence</small></td>
    <td><small>self-organization</small></td>
    <td bgcolor="#ffffff">---</td>
  </tr>
</table>
</p>

<p>
As can be seen, there are many subjects that are clearly related to
more than one book part.  By viewing all of these topics in a
multidisciplinary manner, one can appreciate how the topics of
computation, fractals, chaos, complex system, and adaptation are far
more interesting when considered together.
</p>



